1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Hi. In this video, we'll look at testing for IDaWS.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Insecure Direct Object References are a type of access control vulnerability in which an application uses user-supplied input to access objects such as files, directories, or database records directly.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:34,000
If suitable access controls are not in place, this enables attackers to manipulate these references and gain access to other objects without authorization.

4
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
IDaWS are sometimes confused with other types of access control vulnerability.

5
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Note that the term IDaW specifically refers to vulnerabilities in which an application exposes references to its own internal implementation objects.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:57,000
If you'd like to follow along, we're going to use the user ID control by request parameter lab from our Web Security Academy.

7
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Although we're using a specific lab, the principle of running a sniper attack in Burp Intruder should apply to any application in which you find exposed object references in a URL.

8
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
First of all, we need to identify parameters or other locations in which we suspect an IDaW vulnerability exists.

9
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:25,000
In this example, if we log in using the credentials supplied with the lab, we can see that the URL now shows a query parameter.

10
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:35,000
This strongly indicates that on this site, the user ID is used to retrieve the relevant user's data in order to render the account page.

11
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:45,000
If we now go to Burp's proxy HTTP history tab, we can right click and forward this request to Burp Intruder.

12
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Select the Intruder tab and make sure the attack type is set to Sniper.

13
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Highlight the parameter you want to test and click on Add to set this as a payload position.

14
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Select the Payloads tab and add a list of the test values you want to use in the attack.

15
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The example lab requires a list of usernames.

16
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:15,000
If you're using Burp Suite Professional, you can open the Add from List drop-down menu and select the Usernames list.

17
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Click Start Attack.

18
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Burp Intruder will send a series of new requests, replacing the selected payload position with each username in the list.

19
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Study the responses to look for indications that some of the requests sent in the attack were successful.

20
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:45,000
In this case, requests sent using the Administrator and Carlos payloads received a 200 OK response.

21
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,000
This indicates that you may be able to access the account pages for these usernames without authorization.

22
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:00,000
If the responses to these requests show that the page was retrieved, then the IDOR vulnerability is confirmed.

23
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,000
The IDOR vulnerability is confirmed.

